WEBVTT

NOTE English visual guide. Editorial descriptions of on-screen concepts; no spoken narration.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
A CNN maps pixels to a prediction, measures its error and updates its weights. The animation follows

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.200
an image of the digit seven.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.800
The input is a 12 by 12, single-channel grid. Pixel values describe brightness; intermediate values

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.600
can represent softer edges.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.200
A 3 by 3 filter contains nine learned weights and a bias. It starts with initial values, which

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.400
training can change.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.400
Convolution slides the same filter across the image. At each location, multiply corresponding pixels

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:42.800
and weights, add the results and the bias, and write one output value.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Several filters produce several feature maps. Different learned filters can respond to different

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.000
patterns.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.400
ReLU replaces negative feature values with zero and keeps positive values.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.200
Max pooling keeps the largest value from each small region, reducing spatial dimensions.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.000
The illustrated path is 12×12×1 → 10×10×4 → 5×5×4 → 3×3×8. Deeper features combine information from

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.000
a wider input region.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.200
Flattening rearranges the 3×3×8 feature tensor into 72 values, preserving the values in a fixed

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.400
ordering.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.800
The dense classifier combines those features to produce scores for ten digits.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.400
Softmax converts scores into nonnegative probabilities that sum to one. The largest probability is

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.800
the current prediction.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Cross-entropy penalizes low probability for the true class. For a true-class probability of 0.205,

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.000
the natural-log loss is approximately 1.585.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Backpropagation applies the chain rule through the classifier and feature extractor to calculate

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.000
parameter gradients.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.000
Gradient descent updates weights by subtracting learning rate times gradient.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.800
Repeating prediction, loss, backpropagation and update over training examples can improve

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.600
predictions. Evaluate on held-out data to check generalization.
